<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piero Soderini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1452">1452</a>–<a href="/years/1522">1522</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piero Soderini was a Florentine statesman elected gonfaloniere of justice for life in 1502, leading the republic restored after the expulsion of the Medici in 1494. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> as his secretary he created a citizen militia. When Spanish troops sacked Prato in 1512 he fled into exile, and the Medici returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piero-soderini.jpg" alt="Piero Soderini" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ridolfo del Ghirlandaio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piero_Soderini_(1450-1522),_by_Ridolfo_del_Ghirlandaio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Piero di Tommaso Soderini was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, the son of Tommaso di Lorenzo Soderini, a member of an established family associated with medicine, and his second wife Dianora Tornabuoni, who came from a prominent Florentine family and was an in-law of Piero di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a>. His brother Paolo Antonio Soderini was a statesman and supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-savonarola">Girolamo Savonarola</a>, and another brother, Francesco, was a cardinal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Volterra. In 1481 Piero was Prior of the city, and he later formed political ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-di-lorenzo-de-medici">Piero di Lorenzo de' Medici</a>, who in 1493 made him ambassador to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1502 the Florentines elected Soderini gonfaloniere for life, wishing to give greater stability to the republican institutions restored after the expulsion of Piero de' Medici and the execution of Savonarola. His administration followed relatively moderate policies. He replaced foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> with a national militia. The long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> ended during his government with the Florentine capture of that city in 1509. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a>, author of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/discourses-on-livy">Discourses on Livy</a>, served under him as second chancellor and as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Machiavelli's assessment of Soderini's character changed after the events that removed Soderini from power. Soderini kept a pro-France alliance in Italian politics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1512 the Medici returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> with the help of a Spanish army, deposed Soderini and drove him into exile. He took refuge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orasac">Orašac</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, until the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>, who summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and extended his patronage to him. Soderini lived in Rome for the rest of his life and continued to advocate for the welfare of Florence, to which he was never allowed to return. He died in Rome in 1522 and was buried in the church of S. Maria del Popolo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piero Soderini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1494 a Great Council on the Venetian model gave the vote to some 3,000 citizens, and in 1502 <b>Piero Soderini</b> was made Gonfaloniere for life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1497" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Piero Soderini"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1497">What was here in 1497? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
